        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Linear Pottery culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, chiefly the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the upper and middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the upper and middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, at its widest from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Basin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>; key sites include Vráble and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, Bylany in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, Langweiler and Zwenkau (Eythra) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Brunn am Gebirge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Elsloo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and Flomborn, Hienheim and Rössen</span></div>
