    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The early Linear Pottery culture developed on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, possibly as early as 5700 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5330 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The classical Alföld Linear Pottery culture appeared north of the Körös culture</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The LBK reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> while Music Note pottery appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The settlement at Vráble reached its peak with perhaps 70 longhouses and up to 1,725 people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 5000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The killings of the Talheim Death Pit took place, among the earliest evidence of organised violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">5000–4500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Stroke-ornamented ware developed in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and spread down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 4630 – c. 4550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> trackway was laid across the Campemoor bog in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Linear Pottery culture, from the German Linearbandkeramik (LBK), is a major archaeological horizon of the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, flourishing c. 5500–4500 BCE. It began in the regions of densest settlement on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and spread some 1,500 km along the rivers in 360 years. At its greatest it reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Basin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, but it stayed inland: the northern coasts were left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> peoples living on the Atlantic salmon runs.</p><p>Its origins are debated. The oldest theory derives it from the Starčevo-Körös culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Hungary; at Brunn am Gebirge near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, Starčevo-type pottery gives way to LBK pottery in the same longhouse settlement. A second theory sees it developing from local Mesolithic cultures, citing a gap of up to 500 years after farming reached southern Hungary. A third points to influence from the steppe. Ancient DNA from 2005 onward links its people to Neolithic Anatolian farmers, with Y-DNA mostly of haplogroup G2a, and suggests their descendants have been part of the European population for more than 7,000 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The LBK people settled on river terraces with fertile loess and grew a mix of crops in small plots, a “garden type of civilization” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Gimbutas</a>’s phrase. Their main crops were emmer, einkorn, peas and lentils, with hemp and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> for rope and cloth. They raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> above all, with some goats and pigs, and hunted deer and wild boar. A study of Vráble found that fields were used as pastures to make manure, and a GIS study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> concluded that grazing land, not arable land, limited settlement there.</p><p>The household lived in the longhouse, a rectangular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> building 5.5 to 7.0 m wide and of varying length; one at Bylany was 45 m long. Villages of five to eight houses lay about 20 m apart, and nearby villages formed settlement cells. Vráble in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> had perhaps 70 longhouses standing at once and up to 1,725 people around 5100 BCE. Pottery found mainly in women’s graves suggests women made it, and Gimbutas inferred a matrilocal pattern. Flint from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and obsidian from the Bükk and Tatra mountains were traded across the whole range.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As with all prehistoric cultures, the beliefs of the Linear Pottery people are poorly understood, and how far they formed a system is debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a> read the culture within a mother goddess model inherited from the Palaeolithic, arguing that the female principle predominated and that burial pits lined with stone, clay or plaster may have stood for eggs, the dead returning there to await rebirth. Some of the figurines, however, are androgynous rather than female.</p><p>Burial practice varied. Women and children were buried under the floors of houses, while the remains of adult men are missing from them. Cemeteries came into use at about 5000 BCE, holding 20 to 200 graves in groups that seem to follow kinship; both cremation and burial of the body in a flexed position were practised. Only about 30% of graves have goods: men’s held stone celts, flint tools and Spondylus shells from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, women’s most of the pottery and containers of ochre. These may have been gifts or possessions, but either way they point to differences in standing. At the end of the culture, Herxheim and Vráble show marked changes in ritual practice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The culture is named after its pottery: simple cups, bowls, vases and jugs, at first without handles and later with pierced lugs, bases and necks. Its decoration imitates and refines the painted spirals and bands of Starčevo-Körös ware with incised lines, hence “linear” band ware, though the lines are mostly not straight. The middle phase brought Music Note pottery, whose lines are broken by punctures like musical notes, and the late phase Stroke-ornamented ware, in which rows of punctures replace the lines. Analysis shows that each house had its own pottery tradition.</p><p>The people were also skilled builders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. Longhouse walls were of wattle and daub, sometimes with split logs, under slanted thatched roofs carried on rows of posts, oak being preferred. Timber-lined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> built layer by layer show sophisticated carpentry, and timber trackways crossed bogs, as at Campemoor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> around 4630–4550 BCE. The tool kit used flint and obsidian, with no metal: sickles set with flint blades, the ground-stone “shoe-last celt” used as an adze, and flint-tipped augers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Linear Pottery culture spread through lands partly held by hunter-gatherers. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> it arrived about 5200 BCE among people already producing some food, and the La Hoguette culture to the north-west developed farming of its own; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> peoples evidently did not exclude each other. The trade in flint and obsidian from specialised mining settlements to every LBK region is a strong argument for a unity across the culture’s scattered pockets.</p><p>Conflict was part of its history. The Talheim Death Pit in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, c. 5000 BCE, is among the earliest known evidence of organised violence in Neolithic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; study of its skeletons suggests men of neighbouring groups fought to capture women. The massacre of Schletz happened at about the same time, at a fortified site. At Schöneck-Kilianstädten 26 adults and children were killed by blows to the head or arrows, and Herxheim held the scattered remains of at least 450 people. Other suggested causes are vengeance, conflict over land and resources, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>. Some fortifications facing lands of the older inhabitants were used only briefly.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The population rose fast during the culture, a phenomenon called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> demographic transition. Bocquet-Appel attributed it to shorter intervals between births, and its end to higher mortality, perhaps from new diseases spread by closer contact. After 5000 BCE came a population decline of “enormous magnitude”, with levels staying low for the next 1,500 years. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> the population fell back to its first level while arable land was still free.</p><p>A number of cultures replaced the LBK across its range, without a one-to-one match between its variants and its successors: among them the Hinkelstein, Großgartach, Rössen, Lengyel, Cucuteni–Trypillia and Boian cultures. In eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> the late groups passed into the Tisza-Hérpály-Csöszhalom complex and the Tiszapolgár culture. Genetic studies indicate that the descendants of LBK people have lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for more than 7,000 years and are part of the present-day population. New finds continue: in 2019 two large rondel complexes were discovered east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Linear Pottery culture carried farming across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from about 5500 BCE, its people of Anatolian farming descent growing emmer and einkorn, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> for meat and milk and living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> longhouses. Named for its incised pottery, it ended around 4500 BCE after a period of violence and population decline, and was followed by many regional cultures.</em></p>

